==English==

===Verb===
{{en-verb|come<,,came,come> across}}

# {{&lit|en|come|across}}
#: ''He '''came across''' the street towards me.''
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} To [[change]] [[sides]]; to cross over to work for the opposition.
#: {{syn|en|come over}}
#: {{ux|en|You argued well in court but your firm doesn't pay its lawyers well, so why don't you '''come across''' to ours?}}
# {{senseid|en|seem}} {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To give an [[appearance]] or [[impression]]; to project a certain [[image]]; to [[seem]] or [[appear]] (to be some way). {{+obj|en|:as<q:often>|:like<q:often>}}
#: {{syn|en|come off<id:seem>|come over}}
#: {{cot|en|translate|equate<qq:especially regarding abstractions>}}
#: {{ux|en|How did she '''come across''' when you met with her?}}
#: {{ux|en|How did she '''come across''' to you?}}
#: {{ux|en|She '''came across''' as sharp and well-grounded.}}
#: {{ux|en|A business suit and adequate elocution help her to '''come across''' as the competent professional she is.}}
#* {{RQ:Rushdie Fury|page=36|passage=“Because of the British empire, I mean. On which the sun never sets. There’s no offence intended. That’s what I want to be sure of. That the line doesn’t '''come across''' as an insult to your country’s glorious past.”}}
# {{senseid|en|run across, encounter}} {{lb|en|idiomatic|transitive}} To [[find]], usually by [[accident]].
#: {{syn|en|come upon|run across|encounter}}
#: {{ux|en|In the meadow he '''came across''' a rare flower.}}
# To produce what was desired; to [[come up with the goods]]. {{+obj|en|:with}}
#: {{nearsyn|en|come through}}
#: {{cot|en|come around}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''came across''' with the rest of the money when we mentioned our lawyer.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1929|author=Reginald Charles Barker|title=The Hair-trigger Brand|page=160
|passage="I'll die before I let my grandad pay you that much money!" blazed the girl.<br>"That ain't unlikely either," retorted Shanan, "if ol' Bart Hendricks don't '''come across''' with the ransom."}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic|intransitive}} To [[give in]] and do what is wanted or expected; to acquiesce to something.
#: {{nearsyn|en|come around}}
#: {{cot|en|come through}}
## {{lb|en|idiomatic|intransitive}} To have sex; to give in to seduction.
## {{lb|en|idiomatic|intransitive}} To [[confess]] to something.

